\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Voices from the Harbour: An Oral History of the North Quay Fleet}
\author{E.~Thornton}
\date{May 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Between 2019 and 2024 the project recorded forty-two interviews with
retired fishermen, net-menders, and harbour workers of the North Quay.
One fisherman recalled “the whole quay smelled of tar and
salt” when describing the winter of 1968, and the phrase has
become the unofficial title of the archive. This paper sketches the
fleet's working rhythm, the slang of the quay, and the way the
harbour has been remembered since the fish market closed.
\end{abstract}

\section{The Fleet}
In the harbour's busiest decade, the 1960s, some sixty boats worked
out of the North Quay, most of them wooden trawlers built within a
day's sail of the town. Crews spoke of ``grey-water days'' for the
flat, overcast mornings when the catch came up slow, and the phrase
still surfaces in the interviews half a century later.

\section{They Called It “the Iron Beach”}
The concrete apron east of the market, poured in 1954, was known on
the quay as ``the iron beach'' because the winches rusted faster
there than anywhere else. Net-mending families' workshops lined its
landward edge, and the apron stayed in use until the market closed.

\section{Work and Weather}
Winter trips were short and anxious. The harbourmaster's logbook for
March 1961 recorded \emph{“no catch worth landing”} after
the equinoctial gale, and several crews describe the same season in
almost the same words. By spring the boats were back on the banks,
and the quay resumed its ordinary noise.

\section{Memory and Change}
Since the market closed, the quay has been repaved and the sheds
replaced by flats, and younger residents' memories of the fleet are
mostly second-hand. The interviews show how quickly a working
landscape becomes a story, and how the slang of the quay outlives
the work itself.

\section{Conclusion}
The archive preserves a vocabulary as much as a history. Phrases
coined for weather and work now carry the town's memory of the
fleet, and the project continues to record while the last of the
North Quay crews are still willing to talk.
\end{document}

